#
# One DHCP server per mesh.
#
# The mesh is a single L2 domain, so a second DHCP server answers clients
# before the real one and hands itself out as their gateway. The client then
# has an address, full signal and no route anywhere - a failure that looks
# exactly like a broken radio. Measured on hardware 2026-07-25.
#
# Rule: only the controller serves DHCP. Agents switch it off. A node that
# already carries an explicit setting is left alone (the user decided).
#
# ⚠️ The number matters, and it used to be 91.
#
# dhcp.lan.ignore is not only a DHCP setting - it is also one of the three marks
# that say "somebody made this box a mesh node", read by 89-easymesh-quiet-until-
# role, 991-multiap-genconfig and the ieee80211 hotplug handler. Unset means
# factory. Setting it here, at 91, told all of them the opposite two steps before
# they looked.
#
# Traced through the first-boot sequence on hardware 2026-08-17:
#
#     start                         ignore unset   mapagent=1 mapcontroller=1
#     89-easymesh-quiet-until-role  -> daemons off, correctly
#     91-easymesh-dhcp-safe         -> ignore=1
#     991-multiap-genconfig         -> gate now passes, so it rewrote wireless
#                                      and turned both daemons back on
#
# The box came up broadcasting MAP--BH again, and it looked like the gates were
# broken. They were not; they were answered by us. Running after 991 and 994
# was only ever half the story. The real fix is that the gates no longer read
# dhcp.lan.ignore at all - a mark this script writes on every non-controller
# cannot mean "someone chose a mesh role". Running late is kept anyway, so the
# value is not in place while anything else is still making decisions about it.
#
# One reader does sit between the old position and this one:
# 99-easymesh-no-default-role, which sorts before 995 ('-' is below '5'). It
# reads dhcp.lan.ignore at its line 29, so the order of the two flipped. Harmless
# today - 89-easymesh-quiet-until-role has already zeroed the value that reader
# tests first, so it short-circuits either way - but it is a real dependency and
# an earlier draft of this comment claimed no such reader existed.

# ⚠️ And then the same mistake a third time, from the other end.
#
# "Are you the controller?" is not the question this script needs answered.
# 89-easymesh-quiet-until-role sets mapcontroller.controller.enabled='0' on every
# roleless box twenty-three positions earlier, so what this test reads as "an
# agent, switch DHCP off" is its own gate's work on a factory router.
#
# Measured on hardware 2026-08-18, on a box that had just been flashed and had
# taken no role at all:
#
#     OpenWrt-2g/-5g/-6g/-MLD    all four on the air, correctly
#     dhcp.lan.ignore='1'        set by this script
#     port 67                    nothing listening
#
# A client joins the network the board is supposed to ship with and never gets
# an address. The stray backhaul was fixed and the box still came up broken,
# because the question was still the wrong one.
#
# 🔑 The same rule as everywhere else in this package: ask whether anyone made
# this box a mesh node. One DHCP server per mesh only means anything once there
# is a mesh.
# [ -r ] first: sourcing a missing file ends the script, and an exit that looks
# like success here would drop the "one DHCP server per mesh" guard without a
# word - the exact failure it exists to prevent. Exiting non-zero keeps this file
# for the next boot instead.
[ -r /usr/share/easymesh/boot-window.sh ] || {
	echo "995-easymesh-dhcp-safe: cannot read boot-window.sh - not touching DHCP" >&2
	exit 1
}
. /usr/share/easymesh/boot-window.sh
# exit 1, not 0: a uci-default that succeeds is deleted. Exiting 0 here would
# remove the "one DHCP server per mesh" guard permanently at the firstboot of
# every factory box - so the box that later joins a mesh would never be reached
# by it, and correctness would rest entirely on easymesh-role, easymesh-api and
# mesh-role each remembering to set the value themselves. They all do today.
# Keeping the file costs one uci read per boot.
is_mesh_node || exit 1

[ "$(uci -q get mapcontroller.controller.enabled)" = "1" ] && exit 0
[ -n "$(uci -q get dhcp.lan.ignore)" ] && exit 0

uci set dhcp.lan.ignore='1'
uci commit dhcp
exit 0
